.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBvcs\fR and sub-commands provide a \s-1VCS\s0-agnostic front-end over \*(mS's individual version control system
interfaces. Each command determines which \s-1VCS\s0 controls the current buffer's file (or the current directory) by
calling \fBvcs-id\fR, and then forwards the request to the equivalent command of the underlying interface, i.e.
\fBvcs-status\fR run against a file in a
.Ht git 3
working copy executes \fBgit-status\fR, in a
.Ht svn 3
working copy executes \fBsvn-status\fR, and so on. This allows a single set of key-bindings and menu entries to be used
regardless of which \s-1VCS\s0 happens to control the file being edited.
.PP
This page only describes the behaviour common to \fBvcs\fR and its sub-commands; each command's action, its prompts and
its options are ultimately those of the underlying \s-1VCS\s0 command it forwards to, so see
.Ht git 3 ,
.Ht svn 3 ,
.Ht cvs 3
and
.Ht ClearCase 3
for the details, dialogs and command-line options specific to that system.
.PP
Not every sub-command is implemented by every \s-1VCS\s0 interface; where this is the case it is noted below. If a
sub-command is run against a \s-1VCS\s0 that does not implement it, an
.Ht ml-write 2
message of the form \fC[VCS Error: <vcs> does not support <action>]\fR is reported and the command is abandoned.
Similarly, a few pairs of sub-commands are simply synonyms of one another (e.g. \fBvcs-blame\fR/\fBvcs-annotate\fR)
because the underlying \s-1VCS\s0 interfaces themselves use different names for the same action; these are noted below
too.
.PP
The \*(mS \fBvcs\fR command itself opens the file-browser of the detected \s-1VCS\s0, i.e. it is equivalent to running
.Ht git 3 ,
.Ht svn 3
or
.Ht cvs 3
directly, including the same use of a numeric argument \fIn\fR of zero to open a single directory-only window (i.e.
.BR "0 vcs" ).
Only supported by \s-1Git\s0, \s-1SVN\s0 and \s-1CVS\s0; ClearCase does not currently provide an equivalent browser
entry point via \fBvcs\fR, use
.Ht ClearCase 3
directly instead.
.SS "Identifying the VCS"
.B vcs-id
identifies which \s-1VCS\s0 (\s-1Git\s0, \s-1SVN\s0, \s-1CVS\s0 or ClearCase) controls a given file or directory,
walking up the directory tree as necessary to locate a \fC.git\fR, \fC.svn\fR or \fCCVS\fR control directory, or, for
ClearCase, a matching view record. The result of the last two lookups is cached so that repeated calls for files in the
same directory are cheap.
.PP
All of the \fBvcs-*\fR sub-commands described below use \fBvcs-id\fR internally to select which \fC<vcs>-<command>\fR
macro to forward to, and it is also used by the file-browser's directory-listing file-hook to select the appropriate
\s-1VCS\s0 state format for a directory listing, see
.Ht file-browser 3 .
.PP
\fBvcs-id\fR optionally takes a file or directory path as its argument; if omitted the current buffer's file name is
used. When called with a numeric argument (as it is internally by the other \fBvcs-*\fR commands and by the
file-browser hook) it operates silently, only setting up its result for the calling macro. When run directly by the
user with no numeric argument it instead reports the identified \s-1VCS\s0 on the message line, e.g. 
\fCVCS for "<file>" is Git (git)\fR. If no controlling \s-1VCS\s0 can be identified for the given file or directory an error is
reported and the command is abandoned.
.SS Sub-Commands
.PP
The \fBvcs-add\fR command adds the current buffer or selected items to the \s-1VCS\s0.
.PP
The \fBvcs-annotate\fR command is a synonym for \fBvcs-blame\fR.
.PP
The \fBvcs-blame\fR command opens a window annotating each line of the current buffer or a single selected file with
the revision/commit and author that last changed it.
.PP
The \fBvcs-checkout\fR command checks out (creates) a working copy from the repository. Only supported by \s-1SVN\s0,
\s-1CVS\s0 and ClearCase; \s-1Git\s0 users should use \fBvcs-switch\fR to change branches, or
\fBvcs-revert\fR/\fBvcs-unstage\fR to discard local file changes.
.PP
The \fBvcs-coci\fR command performs a simple \s-1RCS\s0-style check-out/check-in of the current buffer's file, see
.Ht rcs-file 2 .
Of the supported \s-1VCS\s0 systems only ClearCase has this per-file check-out/check-in concept (\s-1Git\s0,
\s-1SVN\s0 and \s-1CVS\s0 have no equivalent lock/checkout model), toggling between checking the file out and
checking it back in depending on the buffer's current
.Ht view 2m
mode state; only supported by ClearCase. If the file is not controlled by any of \s-1Git\s0, \s-1SVN\s0,
\s-1CVS\s0 or ClearCase then
.Ht rcs-file 2
is invoked directly instead, giving equivalent behaviour on a standalone file kept under plain
.Ht $rcs-file 5
control. Directories are not supported by either form of this command.
.PP
The \fBvcs-commit\fR command commits changes made to the current buffer's file or selection back to the repository,
the user typically being prompted for a commit log message; exactly what gets committed (e.g. the index/staging area
for \s-1Git\s0, or the working copy directly for \s-1SVN\s0/\s-1CVS\s0) depends on the underlying \s-1VCS\s0. Not
currently supported for ClearCase, use
.Ht ClearCase 3 's
own check-in command instead.
.PP
The \fBvcs-delete\fR command removes the current buffer or selected items from \s-1VCS\s0 control, and typically the
local file too - PLEASE NOTE THIS CAN LEAD TO LOST DATA!!! \fBvcs-remove\fR is a synonym for this command.
.PP
The \fBvcs-diff\fR command displays the differences between the current buffer or selected local files and the
repository version.
.PP
The \fBvcs-fetch\fR command downloads changes from a remote repository without altering the local files or index.
Only supported by \s-1Git\s0 at present.
.PP
The \fBvcs-gdiff\fR command launches a graphical diff to display changes. Only supported by \s-1CVS\s0 at present, for
other systems see
.Ht %xdiff-com 5
based commands such as \fBsvn-xdiff\fR.
.PP
The \fBvcs-log\fR command displays the commit/revision log history of the current buffer or selected files. Not
currently supported for ClearCase.
.PP
The \fBvcs-move\fR command moves (renames) the current buffer's file or selected items, preserving the item's change
log history where the underlying \s-1VCS\s0 supports this. Not currently supported for \s-1CVS\s0.
.PP
The \fBvcs-pull\fR command is a synonym for \fBvcs-update\fR.
.PP
The \fBvcs-push\fR command updates the remote repository with committed local changes. Only supported by \s-1Git\s0
at present.
.PP
The \fBvcs-remove\fR command is a synonym for \fBvcs-delete\fR.
.PP
The \fBvcs-revert\fR command reverts local modifications back to the repository version, discarding the local
changes. Currently only supported by \s-1Git\s0 and \s-1SVN\s0, not \s-1CVS\s0 or ClearCase.
.PP
The \fBvcs-setup\fR command opens the setup dialog of the current buffer's underlying \s-1VCS\s0 interface, e.g.
.Ht git 3 's
\fBgit-setup\fR; it does not have any configuration of its own, each underlying interface must be set up individually.
.PP
The \fBvcs-show\fR command displays the log message and affected files of a specific commit. Only supported by
\s-1Git\s0 anf \s-1SVN\s0 at present.
.PP
The \fBvcs-status\fR command opens a window showing the state of all the items within the current directory. Not
currently mapped for ClearCase, which names its equivalent command \fBcc-state\fR, see
.Ht ClearCase 3 .
.PP
The \fBvcs-switch\fR command switches to an existing branch, or creates and switches to a new one. Only supported by
\s-1Git\s0 at present; \s-1SVN\s0, \s-1CVS\s0 and ClearCase have no equivalent branch-switching concept for this
command to forward to.
.PP
The \fBvcs-unstage\fR command removes items from the staging area/index without discarding the underlying local
modifications. Only supported by \s-1Git\s0 at present.
.PP
The \fBvcs-update\fR command updates the local working files with changes from the repository. \fBvcs-pull\fR is a
synonym for this command.
.SH NOTES
\fBvcs\fR and sub-commands are macros defined in file \fCvcs.emf\fR. They are a thin dispatch layer only; the
underlying \s-1VCS\s0 interface (\fCgit.emf\fR, \fCsvn.emf\fR, \fCcvs.emf\fR or \fCcc.emf\fR) for a given file's
\s-1VCS\s0 must be set up correctly, e.g. via \fBgit-setup\fR, \fBsvn-setup\fR, \fBcvs-setup\fR or \fBcc-setup\fR, for
its \fBvcs-*\fR commands to function.
